// Build configuration.
//
// Committed. Real values go in IOS/Secrets.xcconfig, which is gitignored and
// included optionally at the bottom -- so a fresh clone builds and runs with no
// setup, just with sync switched off.
//
// THE ANON KEY IS NOT A SECRET. It ships in every binary and Supabase publishes
// it deliberately; row-level security is what protects the data, not obscurity.
// It lives here rather than in the repository because it is project-specific
// configuration, and because a dev project and a production one must not be one
// edit apart from each other.
//
// A SERVICE ROLE KEY MUST NEVER APPEAR IN ANY OF THESE FILES. It bypasses RLS
// entirely. If one is ever needed it belongs in a server, never in an app
// bundle -- anything shipped to a phone is readable by anybody who has the app.

// HOST ONLY, no scheme. In xcconfig, "//" begins a comment -- so writing
// https://abc.supabase.co silently truncates to "https:" and the failure looks
// like a network error rather than a config one. Swift adds the scheme.
SUPABASE_HOST =
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY =

// Optional include: the "?" means a missing file is not an error, so cloning
// the repo and pressing build works before anybody has a Supabase project.
#include? "Secrets.xcconfig"
